"An ontology diagram presents classes, properties, and logical axioms used to describe or infer domain knowledge.\n\nUse when: Review a shared semantic vocabulary and its implications. Explain class restrictions and relationships to domain specialists.\n\nAvoid when: Only a concrete collection of entity facts needs display.\n\nVisual grammar: Class nodes are distinct from individual instances. Labeled edges distinguish subclass, equivalence, and object properties. Restriction nodes or compact annotations express quantifiers and cardinality.\n\nBuild: Select the ontology fragment and the logic or notation used. Define class and property names before adding restrictions. Represent logical axioms explicitly instead of relying on spatial proximity. Group related concepts and route property edges outside restriction text.\n\nCheck: Subclass is not confused with instance-of. Existential and universal restrictions retain different meanings. Open-world assumptions and inferred statements are explained where relevant. Axiom symbols and edge labels remain readable at the intended size.\n\nPitfalls: Reading absence of an assertion as proof that it is false. Using database-style cardinality assumptions without the ontology semantics.\n\nExample: A Parent class is defined through a hasChild relationship to a Person, with the chosen restriction stated explicitly.\n\nAlternatives: RDF graph, Class diagram."
